.\" dbclient(1) for Amix: Dropbear 2026.94 as this package builds it.
.\" From upstream's page by Gerrit Pape and Matt Johnston (MIT, see
.\" /opt/amix/doc/dropbear/LICENSE), rewritten in -man for the system's nroff.
.TH DBCLIENT 1 "1 November 2026" "dropbear 2026.94"
.SH NAME
dbclient, ssh \- small SSH client
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dbclient
[
.B \-tTNfgyz
] [
.B \-p
.I port
] [
.B \-i
.I keyfile
] ... [
.B \-L
.I forward
] [
.B \-R
.I forward
] [
.B \-l
.I user
]
[\fIuser\fP@]\fIhost\fP[^\fIport\fP]
[
.I command
]
.br
.B dbclient
[
.I options
]
[\fIuser\fP@]\fIhost1\fP,[\fIuser\fP@]\fIhost2\fP,...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dbclient
logs in to
.I host
over SSH, version 2, and runs
.I command
there, or a login shell when no command is given.
It is also installed as
.BR ssh ,
the name other programs call (rsync's
.BR \-e ,
for one); its options are its own, not OpenSSH's.
It is the client of the Dropbear SSH programs; see
.BR dropbear (8).
It offers the same algorithms as the server (see
.B ALGORITHMS
in
.BR dropbear (8)),
so it talks to any SSH server of the last ten years.
Of a server's host keys it asks for the RSA one (rsa-sha2-256) first,
which is ten times cheaper to verify on this machine than ed25519, and
then the ed25519 one.
.PP
It authenticates with a key first, then with a password.
The keys it tries are
.B ~/.ssh/id_dropbear
and those given with
.BR \-i ;
make one with
.BR dropbearkey (1).
.PP
At the first connection to a host it shows the host key's fingerprint and
asks whether to trust it; the key is then kept in
.BR ~/.ssh/known_hosts ,
and a later connection whose host key differs is refused.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I command
Run
.I command
on the remote host with the user's shell.
The command starts at the first argument after
.I host
that is not an option.
.TP
.BI \-p " port"
Connect to
.I port
(default 22); also written
.IR host ^ port .
.TP
.BI \-i " keyfile"
Use the key in
.IR keyfile ,
a key in Dropbear's format as
.BR dropbearkey (1)
makes it.
May be given more than once.
.TP
\fB\-L\fP [\fIaddress\fP:]\fIlport\fP:\fIhost\fP:\fIport\fP
Forward the local port
.I lport
through the connection to
.IR host : port .
.TP
\fB\-R\fP [\fIaddress\fP:]\fIrport\fP:\fIhost\fP:\fIport\fP
Forward the remote port
.I rport
through the connection to
.IR host : port
here.
.TP
.BI \-l " user"
Log in as
.IR user ;
also written
.IR user @ host .
.TP
.B \-t
Allocate a terminal (the default without a command).
.TP
.B \-T
No terminal (the default with a command).
.TP
.B \-N
Run no command; for port forwarding.
.TP
.B \-f
Go into the background after logging in (needs a command or
.BR \-N ).
.TP
.B \-g
Let other hosts connect to forwarded ports.
.TP
.B \-y
Accept an unknown host key without asking (a changed one is still
refused); given twice, check no host key at all, which is unsafe.
.TP
.BI \-W " window"
The receive window of each channel, in bytes (default 24576).
.TP
.BI \-K " seconds"
Send a keepalive after
.I seconds
without traffic (0, the default, sends none).
.TP
.BI \-I " seconds"
Close the connection after
.I seconds
without traffic (0, the default, never).
.TP
.B \-z
Do not mark the traffic with the AF21 class of service.
.TP
.BI \-J " command"
Talk to the server through the standard input and output of
.IR command ,
run by the shell, instead of a TCP connection;
.I host
still names the host key.
.TP
.BI \-B " host" : port
Connect to
.IR host : port
through the server and join it to the standard input and output, as
.B netcat
does.
.TP
.BI \-c " ciphers"
.PD 0
.TP
.BI \-m " macs"
.PD
Offer only these ciphers or MACs, separated by commas;
.B help
lists them.
.TP
.BI \-o " option" = value
An option in OpenSSH's form:
.BR BatchMode ,
.BR BindAddress ,
.BR ExitOnForwardFailure ,
.BR GatewayPorts ,
.BR IdentityFile ,
.BR PasswordAuthentication ,
.BR Port ,
.BR ProxyCommand ,
.BR ServerAliveInterval ,
.B StrictHostKeyChecking
.RB ( yes ,
.B no
or
.BR accept-new )
and
.BR UseSyslog .
.TP
.B \-s
Request
.I command
as a subsystem.
.TP
\fB\-b\fP [\fIaddress\fP][:\fIport\fP]
Connect from this local address or port.
.TP
.BI \-Q " what"
List the algorithms of one kind:
.BR kex ,
.BR sig ,
.BR cipher ,
.B mac
or
.BR compress .
.TP
.B \-V
Print the version.
.SH MORE THAN ONE HOP
Hosts separated by commas are reached one through the other: the client
logs in to the first, then through a forwarded connection to the next,
and so on.
Each hop is authenticated end to end; a host on the way sees only
encrypted traffic.
.SH ESCAPES
With a terminal, a newline followed by
.B ~.
ends the connection,
.B ~^Z
puts it in the background and
.B ~R
makes new session keys.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DROPBEAR_PASSWORD
The password to log in with.
Other users can read a process's environment with
.BR ps (1),
so use a key instead where you can.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B ~/.ssh/id_dropbear
The default key.
.TP
.B ~/.ssh/known_hosts
The host keys trusted so far, in OpenSSH's form.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dropbear (8),
.BR dropbearkey (1),
.BR scp (1)
.SH NOTES
A key made by OpenSSH's
.B ssh-keygen
cannot be read here; make a key with
.BR dropbearkey (1)
and add its public half, which
.B "dropbearkey \-y"
prints, to the server's
.BR authorized_keys .
